  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 1259 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nModerate west to northwest flow remains progged across the region \nfor Wednesday through Friday, as upper level ridging builds back \nacross the Southern Rockies and several embedded waves translate \nthrough the stronger westerly flow aloft across the Northern Tier. \nModel data supports drier air starting to filter across western\nColorado and into south central Colorado on Wednesday, with \nanother secondary cold front pushing across the eastern plains \nearly Wednesday, keeping enough low level moisture in place for \nscattered showers and storms across the southeast mountains \nWednesday afternoon, with storms spreading east across the far \nsoutheast plains once again through Wednesday night. Will again\nhave to watch for coverage and intensity of storms around the \nAspen Acres burn area for possible flash flooding. For Thursday, and \ninto Friday, the much drier continues to work into the Rockies, \nleading to only a few possible showers and storms across the far\nsoutheast plains on Thursday, and mainly dry conditions areawide\non Friday. The dry and breezy westerly flow will also develop spotty \nmeteorological critical fire weather conditions across portions of \nthe Central Mtns on Thursday, which continue and spread east into \nthe mid Arkansas River Valley and portions of the I-25 Corridor on \nFriday. As for temperatures, Wednesday looks to be the coolest day \nof the week, with highs mainly in the 80s across the plains, with \n60s, 70s and 80s across the higher terrain. Temperatures warm back\ninto the 90s across the plains by Friday. \n\nFor Saturday and into early next week, models continue to indicate\ndifferences in timing and location of occasional passing wave within\nthe moderate to strong westerly flow aloft across the Northern Tier. \nThis will bring in periods of moisture and cooler temperatures with \npossible fronts across the Plains on Saturday and again on Monday. \nOtherwise, there looks to be another influx of subtropical moisture\nacross the Desert SW into western Colorado, leading to chances of \nof daily showers and storms, especially over the higher terrain\nalong the Continental Divide.",
